{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"a0b16417bb6d03f261fde7a5c369974c05a967265d01637a76d72bea154db5c2","slug":"pages.p074-m07-introduction","location":"/pages/p074-m07-introduction.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Three Ways to Collect Data: Observe, Survey, or Experiment","content_includes_title":false,"authors":[{"nameParsed":{"literal":"City College of San Francisco","given":"City College","non_dropping_particle":"of","family":"San Francisco"},"name":"City College of San Francisco","id":"contributors-myst-generated-uid-0"}],"license":{"content":{"id":"CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0","url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","name":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","CC":true}},"abbreviations":{"ANOVA":"Analysis of Variance","CI":"Confidence Interval","CLT":"Central Limit Theorem","EDA":"Exploratory Data Analysis","IQR":"Interquartile Range","SD":"Standard Deviation","SE":"Standard Error","SRS":"Simple Random Sample","INCREASE":null,"INCREASED":null,"DECREASES":null,"OBSERVED":null,"ROSE":null},"numbering":{"title":{"offset":2}},"exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"p074-m07-introduction.md","url":"/textbook/build/p074-m07-introductio-f5679eef5fa681bf431295dac15e15fe.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Obviously, sampling is not done for its own sake. After this first stage in the data production process is completed, we come to the second stage, that of gaining information about the variables of interest from the sampled individuals. In this module we’ll discuss three study designs; each design enables you to determine the values of the variables in a different way. You can:","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"eVBK9HQwYZ"}],"key":"nqlMIY9Vfq"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Carry out an ","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"brpSZZ45qI"},{"type":"crossReference","label":"observational study","identifier":"term-observational study","children":[{"type":"text","value":"observational study","key":"lmL2cWH8nZ"}],"kind":"definitionTerm","template":"{name}","resolved":true,"html_id":"term-observational-study","remote":true,"url":"/pages/glossary","dataUrl":"/pages.glossary.json","key":"hhve6rg3Wz"},{"type":"text","value":", in which values of the variable or variables of interest are recorded as they naturally occur. There is no interference by the researchers who conduct the study.","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"XQwnMgHY93"}],"key":"KLR88qSXAx"}],"key":"WTHO5UKVjh"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Take a ","position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"key":"woaBC3oP8w"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"sample survey","position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"key":"j6NeaishII"}],"key":"l60JqhpADm"},{"type":"text","value":", which is a particular type of observational study in which individuals report variables’ values themselves, frequently by giving their opinions.","position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"key":"fHdzX8tsV5"}],"key":"MI3WS5lxb0"}],"key":"DNjBtV2z5p"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Perform an ","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"zacSgDqBoB"},{"type":"crossReference","label":"experiment","identifier":"term-experiment","children":[{"type":"text","value":"experiment","key":"lom5w68Kxc"}],"kind":"definitionTerm","template":"{name}","resolved":true,"html_id":"term-experiment","remote":true,"url":"/pages/glossary","dataUrl":"/pages.glossary.json","key":"BCjDLEst0i"},{"type":"text","value":". Instead of assessing the values of the variables as they naturally occur, the researchers interfere, and they are the ones who assign the values of the explanatory variable to the individuals. The researchers “take control” of the values of the explanatory variable because they want to see how changes in the value of the explanatory variable affect the response variable. (Note: By nature, any experiment involves at least two variables.)","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"hT0516hsAW"}],"key":"n0HX50MHCA"}],"key":"xjfI0SZxUK"}],"key":"L6jz9r1cin"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The type of design used, and the details of the design, are crucial, since they will determine what kind of conclusions we may draw from the results. In particular, a key principle of statistics—one that is examined closely when we study relationships between variables in the Exploratory Data Analysis unit—is that an ","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"TzdLKtZ0fo"},{"type":"crossReference","label":"association","identifier":"term-association","children":[{"type":"text","value":"association","key":"bprEvPDgwa"}],"kind":"definitionTerm","template":"{name}","resolved":true,"html_id":"term-association","remote":true,"url":"/pages/glossary","dataUrl":"/pages.glossary.json","key":"jar7XV0eO1"},{"type":"text","value":" between two variables (the variables tending to change together) does not guarantee that a ","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"MuFlMgtbnT"},{"type":"crossReference","label":"causation","identifier":"term-causation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"causal","key":"IodaR4oXAT"}],"kind":"definitionTerm","template":"{name}","resolved":true,"html_id":"term-causation","remote":true,"url":"/pages/glossary","dataUrl":"/pages.glossary.json","key":"VsdfKGcrUL"},{"type":"text","value":" relationship exists. In this module, we will explore how the details of a study design play a crucial role in determining our ability to establish evidence of causation.","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"mPhlcOFUrr"}],"key":"sqIJpG3Vjs"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Here is how this module is organized:","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"key":"ew3bOoBNkg"}],"key":"CCrkwGb7Px"}],"key":"jh2UoVMFpb"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"We’ll start this module by learning how to identify study types. In particular, we will highlight the distinction between observational studies and experiments.","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"wzfuVlKfdb"}],"key":"IgrhNQ6u2Q"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"We will then discuss each of the three study designs mentioned above.","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"rk3OV2PpKQ"}],"key":"DyUV89Gpjb"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"